<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juba I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 85–46 BCE</span> · Also known as Juba I of Numidia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juba I was king of Numidia from about 60 to 46 BCE. In Caesar's civil war he sided with the Pompeians, destroying the army of Caesar's legate Curio on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> river in 49 BCE, but after the Pompeian defeat at Thapsus in 46 BCE he took his own life. Caesar annexed much of his kingdom as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juba-i.jpg" alt="Juba I" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juba_i.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
